  "format": "build",
  "skills": [
    "logic.driver",
    "transistor.saturation"
  ],
  "brief": {
    "story": "Four LEDs (about 36 mA in all) should light from one logic output, but a 74HC pin is only good for about 20 mA. Q1 does the heavy lifting: the gate drives its base through a resistor, and Q1 sinks the LEDs’ current. The base resistor is missing.",
    "goal": "Fit a base resistor from the AND gate’s output (column 5) to Q1’s base (column 21) so all four LEDs carry 6–12 mA while SW1 is held, with the chip’s output under 20 mA.",
    "datasheet": {
      "title": "74HC08 output",
      "rows": [
        [
          "High",
          "about 5 V"
        ],
        [
          "Safe current",
          "20 mA per pin"
        ],
        [
          "Q1 BC547",
          "E 20 · B 21 · C 22"
        ],
        [
          "Load",
          "4 × 9 mA ≈ 36 mA"
        ]
      ]
    }
  },
  "hints": [
    "Q1 needs at least 36 mA ÷ 200 = 0.18 mA into its base; the pin must stay well under 20 mA.",
    "I_B = (5 V − 0.7 V) ÷ R_B. 1 kΩ gives 4.3 mA, 10 kΩ gives 0.43 mA: both fine. 100 Ω would load the pin with over 20 mA.",
    "A 10 kΩ from column 5 to column 21."
  ],
